        <div id="outage-profile" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Start With the Outage You Actually Get
          </h2>

          <p>
            Most generator regret comes from buying for an imagined outage instead of the real one. Before you look at a single spec sheet, write down three things: how long your longest outage in the last five years lasted, what season it happened in, and what stopped working that genuinely hurt. A four-hour summer blackout and a six-day January ice storm call for completely different machines.
          </p>

          <p>
            Utilities publish reliability numbers, and your own memory is usually good enough. If your outages are short and rare, a small inverter or a battery power station covers the refrigerator and internet until the crews arrive. If they are frequent and stretch past a day, you need fuel logistics and enough output to run heat or a sump pump, which pushes you toward a large portable with a transfer switch. If you have lost power for most of a week more than once, standby starts to make financial sense.
          </p>

          <p>
            Climate decides your biggest load. In the North, the furnace blower and its control board are the priority, and most gas furnaces draw only a few hundred watts. In the South, air conditioning is the load that makes outages miserable, and a central unit is the single most expensive thing to back up. Homes on a private well add a 240V pump to the list, and homes with a basement add a sump pump that cannot be allowed to stop in a storm.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then add the people. A household member on oxygen, a CPAP, a refrigerated medication or a powered wheelchair changes the priority list completely. For those loads, silent and indoor-safe battery power is often the first purchase, with a fuel generator behind it to keep the battery charged. Write the list down; you will use it in the sizing step.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE SHORT VERSION</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Match the class to the outage, then match the size to the list.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Short and rare: battery station or small inverter. Long and seasonal: large portable plus a transfer switch or interlock. Frequent and multi-day, or someone at home depends on power: automatic standby, often with a battery for medical loads.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="four-classes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Four Classes of Home Backup, Compared
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every machine on this list falls into one of four classes, and the class matters more than the brand. <strong>Automatic standby</strong> units are permanently installed outside, run on natural gas or propane, and switch on by themselves. <strong>Conventional portables</strong> are open-frame engines on wheels that produce lots of watts for the money. <strong>Inverter portables</strong> produce cleaner power more quietly and sip fuel at light load. <strong>Battery stations</strong> store energy rather than make it.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Class</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical output</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical price</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Best for</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Main drawback</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Automatic standby</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">10-26kW</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$10,000-$20,000 installed</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Long, frequent outages; whole-house coverage</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Cost, permits, annual service</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Conventional portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">5,000-12,000W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$700-$1,600 plus switch</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Essentials or most of the house on a budget</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Noise, fuel burn, manual setup</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Inverter portable</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,800-4,500W</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$500-$1,300</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Quiet essentials, electronics, close neighbors</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Limited output; most are 120V only</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Battery station</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1-4kWh per unit</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">$400-$3,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Indoor use, medical devices, apartments</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Runs out without solar or a generator</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Standby is the only class that works when nobody is home. That matters for a sump pump in a finished basement, for a freezer full of meat during a vacation, and for an elderly parent who should not be dragging a 200-pound machine onto a wet lawn. It is also the only class with no refueling, provided it is on a natural gas line or a large propane tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Conventional portables win on watts per dollar. An 8,000-watt open-frame unit costs about what a 4,500-watt inverter does. The cost is noise and fuel: the engine runs at a fixed speed whether you are powering a refrigerator or a whole panel, so it burns gasoline at a similar rate either way. Inverter portables throttle down when demand drops, which is why a small inverter can run overnight on a tank while an open frame cannot.
          </p>

          <p>
            Battery stations are the only option you can safely run indoors. They are excellent for short outages, overnight medical loads and apartments, but their capacity is finite. A 1kWh station will run a modern refrigerator for most of a day; a 3.6kWh unit buys a couple of days of essentials. Pair one with solar or a small inverter generator outside and you have a quiet, efficient hybrid. Our <a href="/best-portable-power-station/">portable power station rankings</a> go deeper on capacity and chemistry. For a circuit-by-circuit outage plan built around batteries, see our guide to the <a href="/best-portable-power-station-for-home-backup/">best power stations for home backup</a>, and for recharging off-grid, our <a href="/best-solar-generator/">solar generator pairings</a>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing: Running Watts, Starting Watts and the Load List
          </h2>

          <p>
            Every motor-driven appliance needs a burst of extra power to start, often two to three times its running draw for a second or two. That is why generators list two numbers. <strong>Running watts</strong> are what the machine can supply continuously. <strong>Starting watts</strong>, also called peak or surge watts, are the brief overload it can absorb while a compressor or pump spins up. Size against running watts, then check that your single largest motor can start on top of everything else.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Load</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical running watts</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Typical starting watts</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Refrigerator (modern)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">150-800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,200-2,200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Chest freezer</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">100-500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">800-1,500</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Sump pump, 1/3 to 1/2 hp</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">800-1,050</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,300-2,200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Well pump, 1/2 to 1 hp (240V)</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000-2,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">2,000-4,000</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Gas furnace blower, 1/3 to 1/2 hp</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">300-800</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000-2,300</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Window AC, 10,000 BTU</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">900-1,200</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,500-2,200</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Central AC, 3 ton</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">3,000-4,000</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Often 8,000+ without a soft starter</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Microwave</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">1,000-1,500</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same as running</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">LED lights, modem, router, TV, phones</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">200-400 total</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Same as running</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Those are typical ranges, not your numbers. The real figures are on each appliance's data plate, usually in amps; multiply amps by 120 for most household loads or by 240 for a well pump or central air condenser. A clamp meter on the circuit gives the running draw directly. Old refrigerators and pumps often draw more than the table suggests, and a tired compressor can need considerably more to start.
          </p>

          <p>
            Here is a worked example for a common Midwest house with gas heat and a basement. Refrigerator 700, freezer 400, sump pump 1,000, furnace blower 600, lights and internet 400, microwave used occasionally 1,200. Running total about 4,300 watts. The biggest starting surge is the sump pump at about 2,200, so peak demand if everything kicks on together is roughly 5,500 watts. A 7,000 to 8,000 running-watt portable covers that with room to spare, and a 3,700-watt inverter covers it only if you hold off on the microwave.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Sizing Rules of Thumb</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">2,000W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Fridge, lights, phones, internet</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">5,000W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Plus sump, furnace blower, freezer</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">8,000W</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Plus a 240V well pump or window AC</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">20kW+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Most whole homes with central air</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Leave roughly 20 percent headroom. Generators run hotter, louder and less efficiently near their limit, and voltage sags under heavy load can stress motors. If your list lands at 7,500 running watts, a 9,000-watt machine is the honest choice. If central air is the reason the number is huge, ask an HVAC tech about a soft-start kit, which can cut a compressor's starting surge substantially and sometimes lets a large portable or a smaller standby carry it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="connecting" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Connecting a Generator to Your House Without Killing Anyone
          </h2>

          <p>
            There are exactly three safe ways to get generator power into your home. You can run heavy-duty outdoor extension cords from the generator directly to individual appliances. You can have an electrician install a <strong>manual transfer switch</strong> beside your panel with a power inlet box outside. Or you can have an electrician install a listed <strong>interlock kit</strong> on your main panel with an inlet. Standby units use an automatic transfer switch that does the same job by itself.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">NEVER BACKFEED</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Do not plug a generator into a wall outlet or dryer receptacle.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">A double-ended male cord, sometimes called a suicide cord, sends generator power backward through your panel and out onto the utility lines, where the pole transformer steps it up to thousands of volts. Line workers repairing what they believe is a dead line can be electrocuted, and neighbors can be hurt. It also energizes your house wiring with no main breaker protection and can start a fire when grid power returns. It violates the National Electrical Code and is illegal in most jurisdictions.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            What every legal connection has in common is isolation. A transfer switch or interlock physically guarantees that your house is connected to the utility or to the generator, never both at once. That protects line crews, protects your generator from being destroyed when the grid comes back, and protects your wiring. It is not a gadget to skip; it is the reason a portable can safely power hardwired loads like a furnace or a well pump, which extension cords cannot reach.
          </p>

          <p>
            Costs vary by region. A manual transfer switch covering six to ten selected circuits, plus an outdoor inlet and a permit, commonly runs $800 to $1,800 installed. An interlock kit is cheaper, often $500 to $1,200 installed, and lets you power any circuit in the panel as long as you manage the load yourself. Both require a permit in most places, and both should be installed by a licensed electrician. Match the inlet to your generator: a 30-amp L14-30 for most mid-size portables, a 50-amp inlet for the largest.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you are using cords, use outdoor-rated, 12-gauge or heavier cords sized for the load, keep them as short as practical, and never run them through a door or window that then has to stay propped open, because that opening is exactly where exhaust can enter. A cord port in an exterior wall or a properly installed inlet is the better answer. Newer portables with GFCI-protected outlets sometimes trip when connected through a transfer switch; that is a neutral-bonding issue for your electrician to resolve, not a reason to bypass protection. A <a href="/best-whole-house-surge-protector/">whole-house surge protector</a> is worth adding at the same visit, and a <a href="/best-circuit-breaker-finder/">circuit breaker finder</a> makes picking the right circuits for a transfer switch much faster.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="carbon-monoxide" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Carbon Monoxide: The Part That Actually Kills People
          </h2>

          <p>
            Portable generators are one of the leading causes of carbon monoxide deaths in the United States, and most of those deaths happen during and right after storms. A single portable generator can produce as much carbon monoxide as hundreds of idling cars. The gas is colorless and odorless, and early symptoms such as headache, dizziness, nausea and fatigue are easy to mistake for flu or exhaustion. People asleep can die without ever waking.
          </p>

          <p>
            The rules are simple and absolute. Run a fuel-burning generator only outdoors. Never in the house, a basement, a crawlspace, an attached garage, a breezeway or a shed, and never in a garage with the door open, because fans and open doors do not clear the gas reliably. Federal safety agencies recommend at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents. Farther is better, and wind direction matters; the research behind that guidance shows exhaust can still drift inside at 20 feet under the wrong conditions.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Placement Numbers to Remember</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Safe indoor locations for an engine</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">20 ft+</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Portable to house, exhaust pointed away</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">5 ft</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical standby clearance to windows and vents</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">Every level</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Battery-backed CO alarms, near bedrooms</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Install CO alarms that do not depend on grid power: battery-only units or plug-ins with battery backup, on every level and outside every sleeping area. Test them before storm season. If an alarm sounds or anyone feels sick, get everyone into fresh air immediately and call 911. Do not go back in to find the source.
          </p>

          <p>
            Buy a generator with an automatic CO shutoff. Honda calls it CO-MINDER, Champion uses CO Shield, Generac uses COsense, and others have their own names; the common reference standards are ANSI/PGMA G300 and UL 2201. These systems shut the engine down when CO builds up around the unit, which protects against the most common fatal mistake, running in a garage or too close to the house. They are a backstop, not permission to cut corners on placement. Standby generators are installed to the manufacturer's clearance rules, commonly 5 feet from openings, and inspected under a permit, which is one more reason they are the safest fuel-burning option.
          </p>

          <p>
            Weather is the trap. People bring generators into garages to keep them out of rain. Instead, buy a purpose-made generator tent or a ventilated enclosure rated for running operation, or build a simple open-sided canopy well away from the house, and never refuel a hot engine; let it cool first to avoid spilling gasoline onto a hot muffler.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="fuel-plan" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Fuel Planning: How Much, Which Kind, Stored Where
          </h2>

          <p>
            A generator without fuel is a lawn ornament, and fuel is what runs out first in a regional outage. Gas stations need power to pump, and the ones that have it get long lines. Your fuel plan should cover your longest likely outage at a realistic load, not the brochure run time at 25 percent load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mid-size conventional portables commonly burn somewhere around half a gallon to a gallon of gasoline per hour at moderate load, which is 12 to 24 gallons a day if you run around the clock. Most owners do not. Running the generator for two hours every four to six hours keeps a refrigerator and freezer cold and a sump pit under control while cutting fuel use dramatically. A small inverter at light load can run all night on a single gallon.
          </p>

          <p>
            Gasoline goes stale. Untreated pump gas begins to degrade within a month or two, and ethanol blends absorb water. Add a fuel stabilizer when you fill the can, rotate stored fuel into your car every few months, and buy ethanol-free gas if it is available near you. Store it in approved containers in a detached shed or garage, away from ignition sources and never inside the living space. Local fire codes often cap household gasoline storage, commonly around 25 gallons, so check yours.
          </p>

          <p>
            Propane is the easier fuel to stockpile. It does not go stale, standard 20-pound cylinders are available at hardware stores, and a dual fuel portable lets you switch when the gasoline runs short. Expect roughly 10 percent less output on propane, and note that small cylinders can struggle to vaporize fast enough in deep cold under heavy load. Natural gas, used by most standby units, rarely goes out in electrical outages, which is why a gas-fed standby can run for days.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">RULE OF THUMB</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Store enough fuel for three days of intermittent running.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">For a mid-size portable run two hours on, four hours off, that is usually 15 to 25 gallons of stabilized gasoline or several propane cylinders. If your outages regularly outlast that, the math is telling you to look at a standby unit on natural gas or a large propane tank.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="real-cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            8. What a Home Generator Really Costs
          </h2>

          <p>
            The sticker price is the smallest number in a generator decision once you count connection, fuel and upkeep. A $1,200 portable becomes a $2,500 to $3,500 system once you add an inlet and transfer switch or interlock with a permit, a heavy cord, fuel cans, stabilizer, a cover and a CO alarm upgrade. That is still far below standby, and for most homes with gas heat it is the best value on this list.
          </p>

          <p>
            Standby is a different scale. The unit is roughly a third to half of the bill; the rest is the automatic transfer switch, electrical work, gas piping and sometimes a larger gas meter, the pad, permits and inspection. Whole-home 20 to 24kW systems commonly land between $10,000 and $20,000 installed, with long gas runs, propane tank sets and service upgrades pushing higher. Our <a href="/generac-generator-cost/">Generac cost breakdown</a> walks through each line of a typical quote.
          </p>

          <p>
            Running costs matter too. Standby units need an annual service visit, typically $200 to $400 with a dealer plan, plus a battery every few years. They also exercise themselves weekly, burning a small amount of fuel. Portables need oil changes after the first few hours and then on a schedule tied to run hours, plugs and air filters, and the fuel you rotate through your car is not really wasted.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then weigh what an outage costs you. A flooded finished basement can cost more than a standby installation. A freezer of meat, a week in a hotel, lost remote-work income or a frozen pipe are all real numbers. If you have had two or three of those events in the last five years, the generator is not a luxury; if your outages are four hours once a summer, a battery station and a cooler are the rational answer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="readiness" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            9. Keeping It Ready for the Night You Need It
          </h2>

          <p>
            The most common generator failure is not mechanical; it is a machine that sat for a year with old fuel in the carburetor. Varnish from evaporated gasoline clogs the tiny passages in small-engine carburetors, and the unit that started fine at the store will not start in a storm. The fix is a routine, not a repair.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For portables</h3>

          <p>
            Start it every month and run it for 15 to 20 minutes with a real load plugged in, such as a space heater or work light. Keep stabilized fuel in the tank, or run the carburetor dry with the fuel valve off before storage, whichever the manual recommends for your model. Change the oil after the break-in period the manual specifies and then by run hours, and check the level before every outage use. Keep the electric-start battery on a maintainer if the unit does not charge it itself.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">For standby units</h3>

          <p>
            Let the weekly exercise cycle run and watch the app or controller for warnings. Book annual service that includes oil, filters, plugs, battery test and a transfer switch check. Keep the area around the enclosure clear of mulch, snow drifts, shrubs and leaves so the cooling air intake works. After any long outage, have the oil checked; a multi-day run uses up a lot of the service interval.
          </p>

          <p>
            Do a dry run before storm season. Wheel the portable out, connect it through the inlet, flip the interlock or transfer switch and bring each circuit on one at a time. You will find the missing adapter, the dead battery or the too-short cord on a sunny afternoon instead of at midnight in the rain. Keep a printed card by the panel with the order of operations, and teach a second adult in the house how to do it.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="matching" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            10. Which Pick Fits Which Home
          </h2>

          <p>
            <strong>Suburban house, gas heat, basement, occasional multi-day outages:</strong> the Champion dual fuel or Generac GP8000E with an interlock or manual transfer switch covers the sump pump, furnace, refrigerator and freezer. This is the most common situation and the best value on the list.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>House on a well, rural, outages that last days:</strong> you need 240V for the pump, so skip 120V-only inverters as the main unit. The GP8000E or DuroMax handles the pump, and the DuroMax adds enough for a window air conditioner. If you lose power for most of a week every year, price the Guardian 22kW on a propane tank. Small cabins and lighter loads are covered in our guide to the <a href="/best-small-generator-for-home/">best small generators for home</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Southern home where heat is the danger:</strong> central air dominates the load, so the honest choices are standby with load management, or a large portable plus a soft-start kit on the condenser. A window unit in one bedroom on a mid-size portable is a cheaper middle path that keeps people safe in a heat wave.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>Apartment, condo, or someone in the house on a medical device:</strong> start with the EcoFlow Delta Pro or a smaller battery station. It runs indoors safely, and you can add a quiet inverter outside to recharge it if outages stretch. <strong>Close neighbors and short outages:</strong> the Honda EU2200i or the Westinghouse iGen4500 keeps the essentials going quietly. If you prefer straight gasoline and big output, compare our <a href="/best-gas-generator/">best gas generator</a> picks.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            11. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sizing to the wattage number on the box</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The large number on most packaging is starting watts, not what the machine can carry all night. Build your list from running watts, add the biggest single starting surge on top, and leave about 20 percent headroom. A generator run at its limit for hours wears quickly and sags voltage that motors do not like.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Skipping the transfer switch</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Extension cords work for a fridge and a lamp, but they cannot reach a furnace, a well pump or a hardwired sump pump. Plugging into a wall outlet to reach them backfeeds the grid and can kill a line worker. Budget for an interlock or transfer switch, installed with a permit, as part of the generator purchase.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Running it in the garage to keep it dry</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Every storm season, people die from generators run in attached garages with the door open. The gas spreads into the house faster than most expect. Run it outdoors, 20 feet or more from the house, with exhaust aimed away, under a ventilated cover if it is raining, and keep battery CO alarms working.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Storing it with old gasoline</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A tank of untreated fuel left for a year leaves varnish in the carburetor, and the generator will not start when you need it. Use stabilizer every time you fill a can, run the unit monthly under load, and either keep the tank stabilized or run the carburetor dry according to the manual.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying more generator than you can fuel</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A 12,000-watt portable at heavy load can drink a lot of gasoline a day, and stations close in regional outages. Before buying big, figure out where three days of fuel will come from. If the answer is unclear, a smaller unit run in cycles, propane, or a natural gas standby is the better plan.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <PlanTheJob />

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            12. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How big a generator do I need for an average house during an outage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For essentials only, refrigerator, freezer, lights, internet and a gas furnace blower, about 3,500 to 5,000 running watts is enough. Add a sump pump or a 240V well pump and you are in the 6,000 to 8,000 watt range. Covering central air and the whole panel usually means a 20 to 24kW standby unit or a load-managed smaller one. Check your appliance data plates rather than relying on averages.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a standby generator worth it over a good portable?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on how often and how long you lose power. Standby costs several times more but starts on its own, needs no refueling on natural gas, and covers the whole house. If you lose power for multiple days most years, have a finished basement, or someone at home cannot manage a portable, standby is usually worth it. For occasional outages, a portable and interlock is far better value.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How far from the house should a portable generator be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Federal safety guidance says at least 20 feet from the house, with the exhaust pointed away from doors, windows and vents, and farther is better. Never run it in a garage, carport against the house, basement, crawlspace or shed, even with doors open. Pair correct placement with a CO shutoff generator and battery-powered CO alarms inside on every level.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can I plug my generator into a dryer outlet to power the house?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                No. That is backfeeding, and it can electrocute utility workers, injure neighbors, damage your generator when grid power returns and start a fire. It also violates electrical code. The legal ways to power house circuits are a manual transfer switch or a listed panel interlock with an outdoor inlet, installed by a licensed electrician, or extension cords run directly to appliances.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Will a portable generator run my central air conditioner?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Sometimes. A 3-ton condenser typically runs on 3,000 to 4,000 watts but can need several times that to start. A large portable in the 9,000-plus running watt class can often handle it, especially with a soft-start kit installed by an HVAC technician, but there may be little left for anything else. Many households run a window unit in one room instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How long will a generator run on a tank of gas?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It varies widely with load. A small inverter can run eight hours or more at light load on about a gallon, while a large open-frame portable might run eight to twelve hours at half load on a big tank and far less near full load. Check the manufacturer's run-time figures at 25 and 50 percent load and plan your fuel around realistic usage.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is a battery power station a real replacement for a generator?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For short outages, apartments and medical devices, yes, and it is the only option you can run indoors. For multi-day outages it is a supplement, not a replacement, unless you add solar panels or a generator to recharge it. A typical refrigerator uses 1 to 2 kWh a day, so divide the station's capacity by your daily loads to see how long it lasts.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

